  "narrative": "All twelve newly evaluated contrasts remain near the null and none should be accepted. I used the approximate 10%-of-absolute-outcome-mean clinical threshold for each outcome. Because these are unadjusted discovery contrasts with many correlated tests, I required not only a support-direction point estimate but also a plausible interval before treating any candidate as more than a low-priority follow-up. None of the new 95% Welch intervals excludes zero, and none has a point estimate reaching the corresponding clinically meaningful threshold. H14, H19, H20, H16, and H28 are rejected and closed because the support-direction upper bound is below the relevant 10% threshold, so a clinically meaningful association in the hypothesized direction is reasonably ruled out within this dataset. For H20, the negative side of the interval is near the opposite-direction threshold; this only rules out the hypothesized positive/weaker-dependency direction and does not prove that a small opposite association is absent, but the opposite-direction point estimate is small and would be a post-hoc reverse after multiplicity. H13, H22, H27, H21, H25, H26, and H15 remain unresolved only because their intervals still allow a support-direction effect above threshold, but their point estimates are well below the clinical threshold and should not be accepted on this evidence. H13 and H22 are kept active as the most internally coherent candidates: feature_054 level_002 vs level_001 has the same signed direction in H7 and H13, while H14 gives a contradictory null/reject; feature_045 has the same raw direction across H17, H22, and H15, although each interval includes null. feature_002 remains a narrower candidate in the higher feature_024 eligibility range, supported by H9 and partially by H26, while H19 rejects the complementary low feature_024 range; the outcome_001 stratum and interaction claims are too imprecise. H18 is closed despite allowing a threshold-sized interval because outcome_014 has a near-zero mean, making the relative threshold unstable and the absolute effect small. No validation is requested: independent validation would be most useful for a candidate with a support-direction point estimate at or above the clinical threshold and an interval excluding null, or for a pattern replicated across several outcomes; the current candidates have none of these properties, so internal follow-up is more efficient. New proposals test the two best coherence patterns on additional related outcomes (feature_054 on outcome_012; feature_045 on outcome_005 and outcome_010), test the high feature_024 feature_002 candidate on outcome_009, and retain one broad high-prevalence screen on outcome_012.",
